# SLH-I-0002 — Typed links: querying and seeing the edges of the knowledge graph

Status: backlog
Related: [base agent](../../agents/base-agent.hmd),
[wiki index](../../knowledge/index.hmd) (its part "The relations"),
[relations-by-kind query](../../.hql/queries/relations-by-kind.hql),
[question-coverage query](../../.hql/queries/question-coverage.hql),
[agent-coverage query](../../.hql/queries/agent-coverage.hql),
[agent-tools query](../../.hql/queries/agent-tools.hql),
[SLH-I-0001](SLH-I-0001-agents-vault.hmd) (its part on dangling `performs` links);
in the HQL project: the book chapter "Graphs", the wiki card "The link
operator", the `graph` extension (`uplinks`, `downlinks`, `expand`);
in the HMD project: the VS Code extension's graph tab (HMD-0025) and the
TypeScript core's graph

The goal is for HQL to query, and the editor to show, the knowledge graph as
the graph it actually is. That graph has typed edges. Most of what this vault
says lives on the links between cards and not inside them. Today the edges
can be written and read, but only with boilerplate, nothing checks them, and
the editor can't display them at all. This issue starts with edges, because
they are the part of the graph the rest depends on.

## What a typed link is

In a card body,

```hmd
[[induction]] {relation: performs}
```

is one link with data attached. `[[induction]]` is a plain wikilink: an edge
from the card it sits in to the card `induction`. The braces after it are a
map carried on that edge. HQL's link operator card spells out the
expansion: written in `base-agent`, the line means

```hql
@[[base-agent]] -> @[[induction]] { relation: performs }
```

A query sees the map as `e.data`, so `e.data.relation` is `"performs"`. A link
without braces is the same edge with empty data. To a reader the text is
still a link; to a query it is a typed fact.

## Why this vault relies on it

A plain link says only that two cards are connected. The base agent links to
`induction` because it performs it, to `hmd-agent` because it is one, to
`human-in-the-loop` because it relies on it, and to `strange-loop` to say it is
*not* one. Without a type those four links are indistinguishable.

The [wiki index](../../knowledge/index.hmd) fixes the vocabulary: ten kinds
between concept cards (`is-a`, `instance-of`, `part-of`, `example-of`,
`contrasts`, `distinguished-from`, `generalizes`, `uses`, `analogous-to`,
`answers`), plus `performs` in the agents vault. Queries build on those
kinds:

- [relations-by-kind](../../.hql/queries/relations-by-kind.hql) counts the
  edges of each kind, with unlabelled ones counted as `untyped`.
- [question-coverage](../../.hql/queries/question-coverage.hql) follows
  `answers` edges.
- [agent-coverage](../../.hql/queries/agent-coverage.hql) compares the base
  agent's `performs` edges with the cards declared `instance-of`
  cognitive-operation.
- [agent-tools](../../.hql/queries/agent-tools.hql) gives the agent a
  `relations(name)` tool that returns lines such as `"performs induction"`.

## Where it falls short

### 1. Every query re-implements "edges of kind K"

`e.data.relation` is optional, so each query unwraps it by hand:

```hql
match e.data.relation { some(String r) => r == kind, _ => false }
```

This line, or a variant of it, appears in seven queries. `uplinks` and
`downlinks` take no arguments, and `expand` filters by direction but not by
kind. Asking a card for its `performs` edges takes a helper function in every
file that needs it.

### 2. Nothing checks the vocabulary

The relation is a free string. A typo such as `perfroms` creates a twelfth
kind, and every `== "performs"` filter skips that edge without a word. The
vocabulary exists only as prose in the wiki index. No query or schema holds
it. The only defence today is to read relations-by-kind and spot a kind with
a count of 1.

### 3. One pair of cards has one edge, and the last annotation wins

HQL's `build_edges` (in `src/knowledge.rs`) identifies a link edge by its two
ends. Every mention of the same target on a card goes into that one edge, and
the `{…}` maps are merged key by key, with later mentions overwriting earlier
ones. So a card that writes

```hmd
[[feedback-loop]] {relation: uses} … [[feedback-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}
```

ends up with one edge whose relation is `distinguished-from`. The `uses` is
lost and nothing reports it. No card in either vault does this today (checked
on 2026-11-22), but the vocabulary encourages it: a card can easily use a
concept and also want to set itself apart from it.

Relation cards (`knowledge.type: Relation`) are the exception. Each one is an
edge of its own and never merges, even when two of them join the same pair. So
HQL already has a way to give a pair several relations, just not through
inline links.

### 4. Declared relations and passing mentions look the same

Every `[[…]]` on a card is an uplink, whatever section it sits in. A link in
a paragraph is as much an edge as one in a card's Relations list. It just has
no `relation` unless the same target is annotated elsewhere on the card,
because of the merge above. A "Relations" section is a convention, not
syntax. A query can only separate the two by checking whether
`e.data.relation` is present. That works, but it means "declared" and
"annotated at least once, anywhere" are the same thing.

### 5. Edges that leave the vault dangle

The base agent's seventeen `performs` links point into the knowledge base,
which is a separate vault. In the agents vault's own graph they are unwritten
links. [SLH-I-0001](SLH-I-0001-agents-vault.hmd) owns the decision about what a
`performs` link is. This issue only records that, whatever that decision is,
the graph tools must show it.

HQL itself keeps these edges: a link to a card that doesn't exist is still
an edge whose target doesn't resolve. The editor drops them (next part).

### 6. The editor's webview can't see edge data

The HMD VS Code extension draws a graph tab in a webview. Its model loses
everything this vault puts on edges:

- **No data.** The TypeScript core's `GraphEdge` holds `source`, `target`,
  `kind` (`link` or `embed`), the raw text and a position, but no data map.
  The relation never reaches the webview, so every edge is drawn the same.
- **One edge per pair here too.** The graph view treats edges as duplicates
  when their source, target and `kind` match, the same rule HQL uses (part
  3), so a webview that drew relations would still draw only one per pair.
- **Dangling edges are dropped.** Unresolved targets are left out on purpose,
  so in the agents vault the graph tab shows none of the seventeen `performs`
  edges, which are the base agent's main content.
- **The rendered tab:** to verify how `{relation: performs}` appears in
  rendered text: as literal braces, hidden, or styled.

So HQL's parser knows about link data and HMD's editor tooling doesn't. The
two halves of the toolchain disagree about what an edge is.

## Work

Most of the fixes belong upstream. This repository is where the need shows up
and where it gets tested. Issues get filed in the projects that own the code.

1. **Here, runs today:** a vocabulary check query. It lists every edge whose
   relation is not one of the declared kinds, with the card and the link that
   carries it. Because of the merge in part 3, a query can't see an
   overwritten relation. Catching conflicting annotations of one target needs
   a check over the card text, not the graph, until HQL reports them. The declared kinds move from prose in the wiki index into data
   that the query reads. Where that data lives (the index's header, or a small
   file under `.hql/`) is part of the work.
2. **Here, runs today:** one shared `is_kind` / `edges_of` helper used by all
   seven queries, if HQL lets a query library share functions. If it doesn't,
   that becomes part of the HQL request in step 3.
3. **HQL:** filter a traversal by edge kind, for example
   `@[[base-agent]] | uplinks(relation = "performs")`. Also decide whether
   `relation` stays an ordinary data key or becomes a named part of an edge.
   Also how typed links relate to relation cards (`knowledge.type: Relation`,
   in the book chapter "Graphs"), which are the other way the language can
   state a relation. On merging (part 3), ask for at least a load warning
   when two mentions give one key different values. Better still, decide
   whether a pair may carry several relations through inline links, for
   example by keying link edges on (source, target, relation). Whether a link
   can be marked as a declaration rather than a mention (part 4) is the
   related HMD question.
4. **HMD:** carry link data through the core's `GraphEdge`. In the graph
   tab, label or colour edges by relation, merge edges by relation as well
   as by target, and optionally show unresolved edges as stubs. Settle how
   the rendered tab shows the braces.
5. **After 0001 decides:** make cross-vault `performs` edges appear in the
   graph tab in whatever form 0001 settles on.

## Done when

- A misspelled relation anywhere in either vault is reported by a query that
  runs today.
- Asking a card for its edges of one kind takes one step, not a hand-written
  `match`.
- Two relations written on one pair of cards are either both kept or reported,
  never silently reduced to one.
- The graph tab shows the relation on every typed edge, and the base agent's
  `performs` edges are visible in the agents vault.
- Every part that this repository cannot do is filed as an issue in HQL or
  HMD and linked from here.
